---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import RaidzMap from "../../components/RaidzMap.astro";
import WriteHole from "../../components/WriteHole.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D23.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figures, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("raidz");
const V = "module/zfs/vdev_raidz.c";
const L = {
  pad: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 4718, 4729),
  pqr: gh(V, 121, 123),
  parity: gh(V, 2443),
  issue: gh(V, 2445, 2462),
  nskip: gh(V, 699, 700),
  skipPlace: gh(V, 509, 580),
  oneSector: gh(V, 2302, 2303),
  enxio: gh(V, 2522, 2531),
  known: gh(V, 3712, 3739),
  cksum: gh(V, 2767, 2772),
  readAll: gh(V, 3886, 3910),
  numFailures: gh(V, 3523),
  verify: gh(V, 3399),
  einval: gh(V, 3385, 3392),
  ecksum: gh(V, 3403, 3428),
  repair: gh(V, 3166, 3210),
  order: gh(V, 3467, 3490),
  parityChoice: gh(V, 2125, 2155),
  window: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 4844, 4848),
  needIo: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 4878, 4880),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="raidz"
  title="What happens when a disk in my RAID-Z fails? RAID-Z, variable stripes and the write hole · Uberblok"
  description="RAID-Z gives every block a stripe of its own, written to free space, so there is no write hole, and the checksum in the parent tells which disk returned wrong data. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>What happens when a disk in my RAID-Z fails?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          A RAID-5 array cuts its disks into fixed stripes, rows that run across all the disks. Each stripe holds some data and its parity, extra blocks computed from that data, so that any one missing piece can be rebuilt from the others. To change part of a stripe, the
          array writes the new data and then the new parity, in place. If the power fails between those two writes, the
          parity no longer matches the data, and nothing records that. The damage shows much later, when a disk dies
          and its contents are rebuilt from that parity: the rebuilt data is wrong, and no error is reported. This is
          called the write hole.
        </p>
        <p>
          When data changes, RAID-Z never updates a stripe in place. Each block ZFS writes gets a stripe of its own, cut
          across as many disks as the block needs, with parity computed from that block alone. The smallest block takes one disk for its data and one more for each level of parity. A large block takes them all. Like every write in ZFS, the stripe goes to free space, and the old
          version of the block stays where it was until the pool has switched to the new one. A power failure can leave
          a half-written stripe, but nothing points to it.
        </p>
        <p>
          When a disk dies, ZFS rebuilds each block that had data on it: it reads that block's parity along with the rest and recomputes the missing part. A disk can also return wrong data without reporting an error. Parity alone cannot say which disk that is, but the checksum that ZFS stores with the pointer to each block can. ZFS assumes one disk is wrong, then another, rebuilds the block each time,
          and keeps the version that matches the checksum. Then it writes the good data back to the disk that returned the wrong data.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="map">
      <h2 id="map"><span class="n">1</span> Each block gets its own stripe</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The map shows the disks of a raidz2 group side by side, six of them, which the code calls children. Each row
          is one sector, the smallest unit a disk writes (4 KiB here). Block A is 24 KiB: its six sectors of data sit in
          four columns, with two parity columns, P and Q, beside them. Write a 4 KiB block after it and see it take three
          disks, one for data and two for parity. A 128 KiB block takes all six, over several rows. Change the number of
          disks or the parity, and every block is laid out again.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hatched sectors are padding. RAID-Z rounds each block up to a multiple of parity + 1 sectors (3 on a raidz2), so that no
          gap too small to use is left between two blocks. That is why an 8 KiB block on this group takes 24 KiB on disk, as much as a 16 KiB block.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then damage the disks beside the map and read the last block back, one step per press. If the missing disk holds a data column, the read asks for parity and rebuilds that column. With a disk that returns bad data, the first checksum fails,
          ZFS reads every column, and the figure shows each guess in turn until one verifies.
        </p>
      </div>
      <RaidzMap lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="hole">
      <h2 id="hole"><span class="n">2</span> No write hole</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Step through both sides at once. On the left, RAID-5 writes D1′ over D1 and the power fails before the new
          parity is written. Nothing looks wrong until disk 0 dies and D0 is rebuilt from the stale parity. On the right,
          RAID-Z writes the new version to free space. After the reboot the old block is whole, and when a disk dies its
          part is rebuilt correctly.
        </p>
        <p>
          The RAID-Z code only does half of this. When a block changes, it writes the new version as a whole new stripe and never reads or modifies the old one. The
          other half is <a href={roomUrl(lang, "copy-on-write")}>copy-on-write</a> and the
          <a href={roomUrl(lang, "txg")}>transaction group</a> commit, which make sure no pointer reaches a stripe before
          all of it is on disk.
        </p>
      </div>
      <WriteHole lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figures follow five places in <code>vdev_raidz.c</code>. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from
        release zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Cutting a block into columns</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>vdev_raidz_map_alloc</code> builds the map of one block. It counts the block's sectors, divides them
          among the data columns, and makes the parity columns as long as the longest data column. Nothing in the
          arithmetic looks at any other block.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_raidz_map_alloc</code>, vdev_raidz.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={V}
            from={634}
            to={649}
            symbol="vdev_raidz_map_alloc"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 634,
                to: 640,
                text: `<code>s</code> is the block's size in sectors, already <a href="${L.pad}">padded to a whole sector</a>. <code>q</code> full rows of data, and <code>r</code> sectors left over.`,
              },
              {
                from: 643,
                text: `The <code>bc</code> "big columns" get one sector more: the <code>r</code> columns that hold the remainder, and the parity columns, which come first (<a href="${L.pqr}">P, Q, R</a>).`,
              },
              {
                from: 649,
                text: `<code>tot</code>, data plus parity. <code>vdev_raidz_io_start_write</code> <a href="${L.parity}">computes the parity of this map</a> and <a href="${L.issue}">sends every column to its disk</a>. Nothing old is read.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Rounding up the space</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Before the map exists, the allocator reserves space with <code>vdev_raidz_psize_to_asize</code>. It adds one
          set of parity sectors for each row of data, then rounds up to a multiple of parity + 1 sectors. The extra
          sectors are the padding in the figure.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_raidz_psize_to_asize</code>, vdev_raidz.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={V}
            from={2315}
            to={2317}
            symbol="vdev_raidz_psize_to_asize"
            notes={[
              { from: 2315, to: 2316, text: "Data sectors, then <code>nparity</code> parity sectors for each row of <code>cols - nparity</code> data sectors that is started." },
              {
                from: 2317,
                text: `Rounded up to a multiple of <code>nparity + 1</code>. The map calls the difference <a href="${L.nskip}">skip sectors</a> and <a href="${L.skipPlace}">writes them as zeros</a>. A one-sector block costs <code>nparity + 1</code> sectors <a href="${L.oneSector}">whatever the width</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Reading the data columns only</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A normal read of a healthy block asks for its data columns and nothing else. Parity is read only when a data column is missing, or by a scrub (a check of every block in the pool) or a resilver (the rebuild of a disk that missed writes). So a disk that holds bad parity goes unnoticed until one of
          those reads it.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_raidz_io_start_read_row</code>, vdev_raidz.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={V}
            from={2573}
            to={2588}
            symbol="vdev_raidz_io_start_read_row"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 2577,
                to: 2578,
                text: `A column on a child that cannot be read is skipped. It was <a href="${L.enxio}">marked just above</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 2580,
                to: 2582,
                text: `Parity columns are below <code>rr_firstdatacol</code>. They are read only with a data column missing (<code>rr_missingdata</code>), for a scrub or resilver, or when parity is forced. The missing data is then <a href="${L.known}">rebuilt from the first valid parity</a>, P before Q before R (<a href="${L.parityChoice}">the choice</a>).`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Finding the disk that lies</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Every read ends by checking the block against the checksum stored in its parent's block pointer. When it fails and no disk reported an
          error, ZFS reads every column and calls <code>vdev_raidz_combrec</code>. The function
          assumes one child is wrong, then two, up to the parity count, and tries each combination in turn.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_raidz_combrec</code>, vdev_raidz.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={V}
            from={3542}
            to={3558}
            symbol="vdev_raidz_combrec"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 3542,
                to: 3546,
                text: `Reached after <a href="${L.readAll}">every column has been read</a>. The first combination: children 0 to <code>num_failures - 1</code>, inside <a href="${L.numFailures}">a loop from 1 failure to <code>nparity</code></a>. The next ones follow <a href="${L.order}">the order the comment above lists</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 3549,
                to: 3556,
                text: `<code>raidz_reconstruct</code> rebuilds those children's data from parity and <a href="${L.cksum}">checks the checksum</a> <a href="${L.verify}">again</a>. <code>EINVAL</code> means more dead columns than parity can rebuild (<a href="${L.einval}">the test</a>), so it tries one more failure.`,
              },
              {
                from: 3557,
                to: 3558,
                text: `The checksum verifies. The columns found wrong are <a href="${L.ecksum}">counted as checksum errors</a> and <a href="${L.repair}">written back with the good data</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Resilvering only what is needed</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          When a disk comes back after missing some writes, a resilver does not copy the whole disk. It walks the tree
          of blocks, skips those written while every disk was present, and asks the vdev whether each remaining block touches a disk that missed writes. The
          figure answers that question for the last block when a disk is set to missing.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_raidz_need_resilver</code>, vdev_raidz.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={V}
            from={4018}
            to={4035}
            symbol="vdev_raidz_need_resilver"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 4018,
                to: 4019,
                text: `The block was not written while a child was missing writes: nothing to do. The scan has already <a href="${L.window}">skipped blocks born outside that window</a>, then <a href="${L.needIo}">asked this question</a> for the rest.`,
              },
              { from: 4021, to: 4022, text: "A block with at least as many columns as children touches every child." },
              { from: 4024, to: 4035, text: "Otherwise, only if one of the <code>s + nparity</code> children from the first column on missed writes." },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figures leave out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The map runs the arithmetic of <code>vdev_raidz_map_alloc</code> and <code>vdev_raidz_psize_to_asize</code> as
        the code writes it, so every layout and every number is the code's. It places each block right after the
        previous one. The allocator puts blocks wherever a metaslab has room. In raidz1, parity and the first data
        column swap disks in every other megabyte of the vdev. Set the first sector to 256 with 4 KiB sectors to see
        it. The read follows the code's decisions (which columns are read, which parity rebuilds what, the order of the
        guesses, what is written back), but tracks only whether each column is right or wrong, not its bytes. It leaves
        out disks that missed writes (read as stale, not missing), slow disks skipped on purpose, gang blocks, the scrub
        path and dRAID. Blocks stop at 128 KiB and groups at 16 disks, so the map stays legible. RAID-Z expansion, which
        adds a disk to an existing group and gives older blocks maps of several rows, is left for another time.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="raidz" />
</Base>

